<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ValidationRule xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        WIRE-FRAUD PREREQUISITE GATE - STORY 28, PHASE 0 (agent-output/design-transaction-fsd-gaps.md
        §5.1 / §10 Phase 0). Blocks B3 (Send wire request to accounting with verified instructions)
        completing while B2 (Call title company to verbally verify wiring instructions (anti-fraud))
        is still open, and I8 (Send closing wire...) while I7 (Verify wiring instructions by phone
        (anti-fraud)) is still open. Ships against the CURRENT standard-Task checklist model, ahead of
        the Checklist__c/Checklist_Item__c rewrite (Phase 1-5); this whole rule is discarded at
        migration step M5 and is not meant to survive it.

        PLACEMENT - SETTLED 2026-08-31, MOVED FROM objects/Activity/validationRules/ AFTER A REAL
        DEPLOY + FUNCTIONAL READBACK. The Activity-scoped file compiled and deployed clean (17/17,
        zero errors, component changed:true), which this repo had already learned is not proof of
        anything (see DPEG_Task_Edit's 2026-08-28 note on a Task <objectPermissions> block that
        deployed green and was silently discarded). The Tooling query
        `SELECT Id, ValidationName, Active, EntityDefinition.QualifiedApiName FROM ValidationRule`
        returned exactly one row for this rule, `Active=true`, `EntityDefinition.QualifiedApiName =
        'Activity'` - a THIRD outcome nobody had enumerated (Task+Event and Task-only were both
        considered; binding to the abstract parent and firing on NEITHER concrete object was not).
        Confirmed functionally, not just by the describe: two tests that inserted a Task already
        `Status='Completed'` with an unmet prerequisite got `System.AssertException`, not
        `DmlException` - the inserts the rule was supposed to refuse SUCCEEDED. Declaring this rule
        directly under `objects/Task/validationRules/` instead - exactly the fallback shape this
        file itself proposed before that deploy - closes the gap. `objects/Task/Task.object-meta.xml`
        already exists, so no stub object file is needed. Task-only ALSO disposes of the Event
        question entirely: Event has no `Status` field, so `ISPICKVAL(Status, 'Completed')` could
        never have evaluated there regardless of binding.
        ⚠ LESSON WORTH CARRYING FORWARD, NOT RE-LEARNED: `objects/Activity/fields/**` DOES federate
        a field onto both Task and Event - that half of this feature (Blocked_By__c,
        Is_Prerequisite_Met__c) worked exactly as documented and needed no change. Validation rules
        do NOT federate the same way. Fields federate; validation rules do not - do not assume the
        two behave identically again on this platform.
        🔴 THE STALE `objects/Activity/validationRules/Prerequisite_Must_Be_Met_To_Complete` ORG
        COMPONENT MUST BE REMOVED SEPARATELY. Deleting the source file does not delete the deployed
        org component - it must go via a destructive-changes deploy (constructive add of this file +
        destructive removal of the Activity-scoped one, in ONE `--manifest` payload per this repo's
        own measured constraint that `--post-destructive-changes` cannot pair with `--source-dir`),
        executed by the devops/admin deploy step, not assumed to have happened because this file
        exists.

        THE FIELD SHAPE, AND WHY IT IS NOT A LOOKUP.
        Blocked_By__c cannot be Lookup(Task): measured on this org, a CustomField with
        referenceTo=Task fails deploy with "referenceTo value of 'Task' does not resolve to a valid
        sObject type" - Task/Event are not valid custom-relationship targets on this platform. So
        Blocked_By__c is Text(20) holding the prerequisite item's Group_Letter__c + '|' +
        Task_Sequence__c key (e.g. 'B|2' for item B2). Phase 0 seeds it via a hardcoded pair map in
        the fan-out service (B3->B2, I8->I7 only, added by the developer agent) - never a
        subject-string match (that trap is documented at length in the design doc's §2.8/§5.1).
        `Transaction_Task_Def__mdt` has no column to drive this generically today; adding one and
        generalising past the two hardcoded pairs is Phase 1, not this change.

        WHY A VALIDATION RULE AND NOT JUST THE TRIGGER.
        TaskRollupTriggerHandler's existing before-update context resolves Blocked_By__c against its
        sibling Task (matched by Task_Group__c + Task_Sequence__c under the same Transaction_Deal__c)
        and stamps the denormalised Is_Prerequisite_Met__c checkbox - that covers the UI, the API, a
        Flow and a data loader. But this repo has already made the call, on Wire__c's
        Verified_DateTime_Not_Backdatable, that FLS/trigger coverage alone is insufficient (D-12 =
        BOTH): a validation rule STILL evaluates under AccessLevel.SYSTEM_MODE, where a bare `update`
        from a future automation path would otherwise slip through untouched. A validation rule cannot
        read a sibling record directly, which is exactly why Is_Prerequisite_Met__c exists as a
        denormalised backstop for THIS rule to read.

        WHY OR(ISNEW(), ISCHANGED(Status)).
        ISCHANGED(Status) is FALSE on insert (Wire__c's same precedent), so an ISCHANGED-only rule
        would let a data loader or API caller INSERT a Task pre-set to Status = 'Completed' straight
        through with no evaluation at all. ISNEW() closes that gap. Symmetrically, this means the rule
        does NOT re-trap an already-Completed row for an unrelated field edit (e.g. a Comment change)
        made after completion - only an actual transition into, or an insert directly as, Completed is
        gated. That mirrors the Wire__c precedent's reasoning for not trapping pre-existing records.

        WHY THE SURVIVING-KEY CHECK, NOT A BARE NOT(ISBLANK(Blocked_By__c)) - CODE REVIEW FIX,
        2026-08-31. The original conjunct read the NEW value of Blocked_By__c only, which a single
        `update new Task(Id = b3Id, Status = 'Completed', Blocked_By__c = null)` defeats: the same DML
        statement both completes the item AND clears the very key the rule is meant to check, so
        `NOT(ISBLANK(Blocked_By__c))` reads blank and the rule never fires. The trigger has the same
        hole and the developer agent is closing it there as the PRIMARY control; this rule is the
        SYSTEM_MODE backstop and needs the identical hardening, or the backstop is defeated by exactly
        the write path it exists to catch. Fix: consult whichever of the new or prior value is
        non-blank - `IF(ISNEW(), Blocked_By__c, BLANKVALUE(Blocked_By__c, PRIORVALUE(Blocked_By__c)))`.
        On INSERT there is no prior value to consult at all, so the ISNEW() branch reads the new value
        only, deliberately never calling PRIORVALUE() in that branch rather than assuming what an
        undocumented no-prior-value case would return. On UPDATE, BLANKVALUE prefers the surviving new
        value and falls back to the prior one only when the new value is blank - so a save that clears
        Blocked_By__c to null for a genuinely legitimate reason (the developer's completion service
        clearing it after a prerequisite is honestly satisfied, if that ever becomes a real write path)
        is not distinguished from an attempt to null it out to dodge the gate; both are refused if the
        record is simultaneously going to Completed with an unmet prerequisite, which is the entire
        point of a backstop.
        PRIORVALUE() AVAILABILITY - MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. `PRIORVALUE(Blocked_By__c)` was proven by a
        check-only dry-run (usman-dpeg, 2026-08-31) to compile with zero errors in a ValidationRule on
        this exact Text field, once the field itself was included in the same deploy package (a first,
        field-isolated probe falsely reported "Field Blocked_By__c does not exist" only because the
        field wasn't part of that narrower payload - not because PRIORVALUE was rejected). ISCHANGED,
        documented alongside PRIORVALUE with the same availability list, already works correctly in
        this rule and in the proven Wire__c precedent, which is corroborating evidence the compile
        result reflects real support rather than a lenient parser. THIS RULE'S PLACEMENT MOVED SINCE
        THAT PROBE, BUT THE PROBE ITSELF STILL STANDS: `PRIORVALUE` was proven to compile on a Text
        field in a ValidationRule at all, independent of which sObject the rule ends up bound to - the
        Activity-vs-Task finding above is about BINDING, not about whether this function or this
        formula shape compiles. A functional (non-dry-run) readback proving PRIORVALUE evaluates as
        intended at save time on THIS Task-scoped rule specifically has not yet been performed.

        VERIFIED THIS RULE DOES NOT BREAK A LEGITIMATE COMPLETION.
        Is_Prerequisite_Met__c is stamped by the SAME before-update context, earlier in the same save,
        so by the time this rule evaluates it already reflects the sibling's live state - a
        legitimately unblocked completion (no Blocked_By__c surviving this save, or its target already
        Completed) always finds Is_Prerequisite_Met__c = true and saves normally.

        errorDisplayField -> Status, the field the user is actually trying to change.
    -->
    <fullName>Prerequisite_Must_Be_Met_To_Complete</fullName>
    <active>true</active>
    <description>Blocks completing a wire-fraud-gated checklist item (e.g. B3, I8) while its verbal-verification prerequisite (B2, I7) is still open, including an attempt to clear Blocked_By__c in the same save. See the in-file comment for the full Story 28 rationale.</description>
    <errorConditionFormula>AND(
    OR(ISNEW(), ISCHANGED(Status)),
    ISPICKVAL(Status, &apos;Completed&apos;),
    NOT(ISBLANK(IF(ISNEW(), Blocked_By__c, BLANKVALUE(Blocked_By__c, PRIORVALUE(Blocked_By__c))))),
    NOT(Is_Prerequisite_Met__c)
)</errorConditionFormula>
    <errorDisplayField>Status</errorDisplayField>
    <errorMessage>This item cannot be marked Completed until its required prerequisite checklist item (wire-fraud verification) has been completed first.</errorMessage>
</ValidationRule>
